     <div>
      <div id="tt_recapn">
        <h2>Recap</h2>
        <!--
        <p>
          In the interim, the HTF options was compared and water was selected. The methods of thermal transmission was also considered, with pumps being the most feasible. The pump was also proven to allow thermal transmission. Lastly, heat loss was identified in the pipes and different insulating materials were compared, settling with __ as optimal insulating material.
        </p>
        -->
        <p>
          Summary of work done in interim is as follows:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Heat Transfer Fluid (HTF) were compared and water was chosen</li>
          <li>Method of thermal transmission was assessed and pump was proven to be able to transfer heat</li>
          <li>Heat loss was identified and insulative materials were compared with PUR/PIR being the optimal choice.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          In this semester, the installation of pumps and implementation of insulation was required in the next semester.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <div id="tt_insulation">
        <h2>Insulation</h2>
        <p>
          In this section, we compare the PUR/PIR insulation selected in the interim with the commonly used airconditioning insulation (material: XX), and translucent insulating material (TIM) style insulation (explained later). The choice of insulations and its implementation are explained below.
        </p>
        <p>
          PUR/PIR insulation is selected in the interim, comparing the cost of installation, durability and XX. For the project, we use spray PUR/PIR foam with an Aluminium duct (7mm diameter) to encapsulate the insulation. <!--Insert image, side by side with dimensions-->
        </p>
        <p>
          Airconditioning insulation is a common material and can be bought off the shelves, reducing manufacturing time. For this experiment, we use XX type of airconditioning insulation. <!--Insert image-->
        </p>
        <p>
          TIM prevents heat loss, while allowing irradiance to heat the absorber (pipe with HTF). To enable this, the insulation is tranparent, allowing transmission of irradiance. It also has a specific configuration between layers made to prevent convection of air between layers by forming pockets of stagnant air (see figure XX), which loses less heat. Using the concept of TIM, (single layer) translucent corrugated plastic board is used to wrap around the absorber (hose) to improve irradiance transmission and reduce convection of air. This insulation is termed as 'Translucent insulation'. <!--Insert image-->
        </p>
        <h3>Insulation Experiment</h3>
        <p>
          The details and setup of the insulation experiment are below.
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/insulation-details.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Details of insulation experiment"></image-component>
        <p>
          The experiment was repeated to verify results. Between the 3 experiment environments (shade, sunlight, and sunlight to shade), the sunlight moving to shade is the most reliable choice due to the application of the insulation outdoors, where it is will not be subjected to sunlight 24 hours a day. However, the shade and sunlight experiments are also reliable in assessing and comparing performance in the 2 conditions. Furthermore, the impact of light on the translucent insulation can be assessed using the shade and sunlight experiments. The results of the experiments are below.
        </p>
        <h4>In shade</h4>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/insulation-shade.jpg"
          subtitle="Figure XXX: Results of experiment in shade"></image-component>
        <p>
          The PUR/PIR insulation and aircon insulation performed best, being similar at the start, with similar gradients, until just under 50ºC, where the PUR/PIR insulation is seen to have lower gradient indicating lower rate of heat loss. The translucent insulation did not perform as well as the PUR/PIR and aircon insulation at high temperatures (60ºC), but as the temperature fell, the rate of heat loss improved and the water took the longest time to reach equilibrium. (XX: To add tangents to the graph to show the change between the different sections)
        </p>
        <p>
          As the design specifications indicates ideal maximum temperature circulating to be 60ºC, we focus on performance at the range of 60-40ºC to ensure temperature is over 40ºC for the longest duration. Between the different insulations, PUR/PIR is the best option in the shade, considering the rate of heat loss. Furthermore, considering the duration the temperature is held above 40ºC, PUR/PIR performed the best.
        </p>
        <h4>Under sunlight</h4>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/insulation-sun.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Results of experiment in mainly indirect sunlight"></image-component>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/insulation-sun.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Results of experiment in mainly direct sunlight"></image-component>
        <p>
          When conducting the experiments under sunlight, it is observed that the results differ under different conditions. When conducted under mainly direct sunlight, the aircon insulation performed better with a lower rate of heat loss, whereas in indirect sunlight, the PUR/PIR insulation performed best with a lower rate of heat loss. This is theorised to be a result of the different absorptivity between the aircon insulation and the PUR/PIR insulation. As the aircon insulation is of black colour, it has a high absorptivity of 0.9 [XX: cite], whereas the aluminium, which covers the PUR/PIR insulation, has an absorptivity of 0.2 [XX: cite], and it mainly reflects light. Between the two insulations, PUR/PIR insulation will thus have lower energy absorbed than the aircon insulation. This is verified as the PUR/PIR is more stable temperature under sunlight compared to the aircon insulation. [XX: verify with the extended period (between indirect and direct sunlight)] This accounts for the variations between the aircon and PUR/PIR insulation results conducted under sunlight.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also noted that the translucent insulation also reaches a higher equilibrium temperature than the PUR/PIR insulation, although the rate of heat loss at the start of the experiment (high temperature) was lower than both PUR/PIR and aircon insulation. This can be accounted by the absorption of the irradiance by the water which is possible by the transmission of the irradiance thorugh the translucent material.
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/insulation-sun.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Results of experiment in direct/indirect sunlight for 5 hours"></image-component>
        <h4>From sunlight to shade (XX: Insert all 3 experiments)</h4>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/insulation-sun-shade.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Results of experiment from sunlight to shade"></image-component>
        <p>
          In the experiment conducted testing from sunlight to shade, the same trend as the under sunlight was observed, where in direct sunlight, the aircon insulation performed best and in indirect sunlight, the PUR/PIR insulation performed best.
        </p>
        <h4>Conclusion</h4>
        <p>
          To select the optimal insulation, it depends on the context of the applications. On the rig, the insulation will be largely underneath the reflectors (XX: Insert image), which will block direct sunlight and will be largely shaded. Furthermore, the application is in Tanjong Balai, which receives mainly indirect sunlight. As such, for the section of pipe above the reflectors, the result from the indirect sunlight will matter more. Overall, for both cases, the PUR/PIR insulation is the best option considering the results above.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div id="tt-integration">
        <h2>Integration of system</h2>
        <h3>Initial integration</h3>
        <p>
          In the integration, the length of pipe is run thorough the centre X frame and to the TES (with XX <i>l</i> of water) and pump in the configuration below. The length of the pipe was kept long in the initial integration as movement of the TES is expected. The length will be subsequently shortened.
        </p>
        <p>
          The insulation does not cover the entire length of the pipe. The green pipe is unable to be insulated as the pipe cannot be damaged and the PUR/PIR insulation is not flexible enough to cover insulation for the other areas. This will be addressed later when the TES is able to be fixed, and this section is to see the impact of insulation installation. The image of the system uninsulated and insulated using PUR/PIR insulation is seen below.
        </p>
        <p>
          In this section, we observe the effect of the insulation on the temperature of the water in the TES. Below are the temperature of water in the TES for the uninsulated and insulated system with the long pipe for both cases.
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/integrated-1-uninsulated.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Temperature of water in TES and in control for uninsulated pipes"></image-component>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/integrated-1-insulated.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Temperature of water in TES and in control for insulated pipes"></image-component>
      </div>
      <p>
        The integration of the PUR/PIR insulation on the pipes increased the maximum temperature difference by XX and lengthened the time the water is kept above 40ºC.
      </p>
      <h4>Isolation of the TES from the receiver</h4>
      <p>
        However, it is observed that the temperature dropped drastically past XX time. This is suspected to be due to the pump running constantly, which causes loss of heat thorugh the receiver. To verify and rectify this, we isolate the TES from the receiver system past XX time thorugh:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>the installation of a relay, which shuts off power to the pump from XX to XX</li>
        <li>the lifting of the TES inlet pipe from the surface of the water in the TES</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <i>Insert images to explain</i>
      </p>
      <p>
        By doing so, we prevent heat loss by conduction up the hose to the receiver thorugh the isolation of the inlet pipe and the peristaltic pump. The results of this change is below. It is also to note that the volume of water in the TES was reduced to XX<i>l</i> so only the rate of heat loss can be compared.
      </p>
      <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/integrated-2-isolation.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Temperature of water in TES and in control when isolating systems"></image-component>
      <p>
        Based on the results, the isolation improves the rate of heat loss past XX time as seen by the gentler gradient in the temperature of the water in the TES.
      </p>
      <h4>Shortening of the pipe and improved insulation</h4>
      <p>
        Due to the length of the pipe and the gaps between the insulation, there are losses in thermal energy. This can be addressed by fixing the TES underneath the middle X frame, which will have the shortest length of pipe required. For areas the PUR/PIR are unable to cover under the reflectors, aircon insulation will be used as it is the second best performing insulation under shade and is flexible enough to cover the hose.
      </p>
      <p><i>insert image of the layout</i></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div id="tt_transmission_methods">
        <h2>Testing methods of thermal transmission</h2>
        <p>
          In the interim, pumps have been proven to allow thermal transmission. In this section, we assess thermosyphons in the system, to assess the importance of height differences and its potential for usage in the CSP, provided that the TES can be placed outside the 1x1m rig size. (insert rough image of what it will look like. better to explain in image than words)
        </p>
        <p>
          In small scale tests, we assessed if flow is observed in standard thermosyphon methods and if a removal of height difference allowed flow. The tests indicate that the height difference of 0m was unable to allow flow, which can be observed in standard thermosyphons as comparison and control. As such, to use passive methods, height difference is necessary.
        </p>
        <p>
          However, in our TES, the pipe are unable to be laid out exactly as the standard thermosyphon small scale tests. Hence, we test a small scale system using the limitations of our prototype rig (namely, hose fed through top of TES and heated section as coil).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <div id="tt_system_size">
        <h1>Optimal size of system</h1>
        <p>
          In the final design, a system of rigs will be utilised. To assess the optimal size per system, we consider:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Heat loss across the system, set at threshold of XX% (XX: cite), and</li>
          <li>System requirements (head and flow rate), comparing to exising pumps</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The system of rigs are analysed based on these configurations, which are capable of tesellation, allowing optimal usage of land area.
        </p>
        <p>
          From this, we also determine the best system connectivity plan and pipe size based on:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Heat loss</li>
          <li>System head, linking to pump availability</li>
          <li>Cost (pump and pipe) XX</li>
        </ol>
        <h2>System layout</h2>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/rig-layout.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Diagram of system rigs layout for different number of rigs"></image-component>
        <h3>Heat loss along pipe</h3>
        <p>
          We compare 2 sets of layout:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Separate loop, where each rig has its own pump</li>
          <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/separate-loop-diagram.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Diagram of pipe system for separate loop system"></image-component>
          <li>Single loop, where a single loop runs through the entire system. (see diagram below)</li>
          <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/single-loop-diagram.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Diagram of pipe system for single loop system"></image-component>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Based on the length required per system and per layout, we plotted the total efficiency of the pipes to the number of rigs based on the 5% heat loss assumption along 3m pipe. The layout are as follows:
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/separate_loop_total_efficiency.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Efficiency vs number of rigs in separate loop system"></image-component>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/single_loop_total_efficiency.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Efficiency vs number of rigs in single loop system"></image-component>
        <p>
          Based on the area available in the hotel (0.5 hectares) and the approximate layout of the building, we can estimate the number of systems to fit in the hotel and its layout for the different system rig size (see below for example and appendix for all). Using this, the pipe efficiency of the entire system can be estimated using the heat loss approximation above.
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/system-layout.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Layout of system of rigs (size=5)"></image-component>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/inter-system-efficiency.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Efficiency vs number of rigs between system and hot water tank"></image-component>
        <p>
          The threshold of the entire system is set at XX% to meet the lowest loss in commercial heating (cite XX). As such the overall heat loss of the combined system is obtained (see below XX), with the threshold indicating system size of <math><mo>&le;</mo></math>XX to meet the requirements.
          <!--
          The threshold of the pipe efficiency is set at 80% (or 0.8). Between the 2 systems, the single loop is capable of having greater number of rigs than the separate loop for the same drop in efficiency, which is related to the total length of piping required. Overall, a single loop system of rigs <math><mo>&le;</mo></math>6 meets the requirements set under heat loss.
          -->
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/overall-system-efficiency.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Efficiency vs number of rigs in entire system"></image-component>

        <h3>System requirements</h3>
        <p>
          In this section, we assess the flow rate for 5ºC temperature rise, net positive suction head available (NPSHa), and head of the separate and single loop systes. For each of these systems, we consider 3 pipes to replace the rubber hose pipes which is not able to handle high pressures when connected to water mains:
          <!--From the prior section, single loop system is more efficient than separate in reducing heat loss. As such, this section will focus on single loop system only. -- alter: consider both systems BUT the seprate loop system is maximum system head per number of rigs. This one use with the Grothen to compare
        </p>
        <p>
          In the final system, rubber hose pipes and clamps will not be able to handle high pressures associated with water mains connections. As such, the materials required will be altered:-->
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Rubber hose pipes <i>to</i> stainless steel pipes (to compare size)</li>
          <ol>
            <li>1/8" nominal bore (smallest size)</li>
            <li>3/4" nominal bore</li>
            <li>1" nominal bore</li>
          </ol>
          <li>Clamps <i>to</i> associated fittings like couplers, reducers, enlargers</li>
          <li>Additional fittings like tank connectors, 90º bends</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Flow rate</h4>
        <p>
          The flow rate is calculated for each system size based on the power (XXX) from interim for a total of 5ºC rise from inlet to outlet of TES tank. The calculations ignores heat loss along the pipe. The flow rate vs size of rig is as seen below.
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/single_loop_flow_rate.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Flow rate (l/min) vs number of rigs in single loop system"></image-component>
        
        <h4>NPSH available</h4>
        <p>
          For usage in centrifugal pumps, the Net Positive Suction Head (NPSH) available is calculated for each system using the flow rate above and by assuming the K-values and friction factor (0.02). From these, 2 plots of NPSH available curve are plotted on the head versus flow rate graph for the 1/8 and the 3/4 size pipe.
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/npsha_1_8.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: NPSH available for 1/8 pipe"></image-component>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/npsha_3_4.jpg"
        subtitle="Table XXX: NPSH available for 3/4 pipe"></image-component>

        <h4>Head required</h4>
        <p>
          The head of each system is calculated using the flow rate above and by assuming the K-values of the fittings and friction factor of the flow (0.02). Two plots are generated of the head required versus the flow rate required, for the 1/8", 3/4" size and 1" pipe.
        </p>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/sys_head_1_8.jpg"
          subtitle="Table XXX: Head of system vs flow rate for 1/8 pipe"></image-component>
        <image-component tag="image" source="assets/tt_final/sys_head_3_4.jpg"
        subtitle="Table XXX: Head of system vs flow rate for 3/4 pipe"></image-component>
      </div>
      <h4>Ideal pipe size</h4>
      <!--
      <p>
